{
  "description": "Get inference guidance for discovering spec entities from code",
  "instruction": "## Inference Workflow Protocol\n\n### Step 1: Start Session\nCall `specforge.infer_session` with `action: \"start\"` and `agent: \"<your-id>\"`.\nOptionally set `source_roots` to limit scanning scope.\n\n### Step 2: Check Progress\nCall `specforge.infer_progress` to see unanalyzed files and current project.\n\n### Step 3: For Each Source File\n1. Read the source file\n2. Identify entities (behaviors, types, events, etc.) using entity kind guides\n3. Write a `.spec` file with the discovered entities\n4. Call `specforge.validate` to check for errors — fix any before proceeding\n5. Call `specforge.infer_session` with `action: \"mark_analyzed\"`, `source_file`, and `entities_produced`\n\n### Step 4: Validate Continuously\nAfter every 3-5 files, call `specforge.validate` to catch cross-file issues.\nUse `specforge.search` to find existing entities and avoid duplicates.\nUse `specforge.query` to check how new entities connect to the graph.\n\n### Step 5: End Session\nCall `specforge.infer_session` with `action: \"end\"` and the `session_id` from Step 1.\nUse `status: \"completed\"` when done, or `status: \"paused\"` to resume later.\n\n### Retry Pattern\nIf validation fails, fix the .spec file and re-validate. Do not skip errors.\nIf a file has no identifiable entities, still mark it as analyzed with an empty `entities_produced`.\n",
  "layout": "turns",
  "payload": {
    "installed_kinds": [
      "feature",
      "journey",
      "deliverable",
      "milestone",
      "module",
      "term",
      "persona",
      "channel",
      "release",
      "behavior",
      "invariant",
      "event",
      "type",
      "port"
    ],
    "scope": "workflow",
    "tools": [
      "specforge.infer_session",
      "specforge.infer_progress",
      "specforge.validate",
      "specforge.query",
      "specforge.search",
      "specforge.schema"
    ]
  },
  "roles": [
    "user",
    "user"
  ]
}
